# Satori config -- copy to ~/.config/satori/config
#
# This file restates Satori's built-in bindings exactly, so copying it as-is
# changes nothing. It is a starting point to edit, and a record of what you are
# running without reading the source.
#
# A config file MERGES over the built-ins: a chord you do not mention stays
# bound. Deleting a line here therefore does not unbind it -- use `none` for
# that. See docs/CONFIG.md for the format, the full action list, and the
# modifier spellings.
#
# Reload with Mod+Shift+R, or `pkill -HUP satori`. If this file does not parse,
# the bindings you are already running are kept.

# Mod is super/logo. Mod+Alt is reserved for the app lookup at the bottom.

bind Mod+Return         spawn foot
bind Mod+Space          spawn fuzzel

bind Mod+Q              close
bind Mod+F              fullscreen
bind Mod+Shift+Space    float

bind Mod+J              focus-next
bind Mod+K              focus-prev

bind Mod+Shift+R        reload

# Suspends passthrough for the focused window, and turns it back on. See the
# `passthrough` block at the bottom.
bind Mod+Shift+P        passthrough

# The only way out of the session, and it does not confirm. Keep something
# bound to `exit` -- Satori owns every binding, so without one there is no way
# to log out.
bind Mod+Shift+E        exit

# Media keys. These are the only bindings with no modifier, and the only ones
# that may be: XF86 keysyms produce no text, so grabbing them swallows nothing.
# An unmodified letter would be unusable everywhere in the session.
#
# Volume goes through wpctl (wireplumber). `-l 1.0` caps at 100% -- without it
# wpctl boosts past unity, which clips rather than gets louder.
bind XF86AudioMute        spawn wpctl set-mute @DEFAULT_AUDIO_SINK@ toggle
bind XF86AudioLowerVolume spawn wpctl set-volume @DEFAULT_AUDIO_SINK@ 5%-
bind XF86AudioRaiseVolume spawn wpctl set-volume @DEFAULT_AUDIO_SINK@ 5%+ -l 1.0
bind XF86AudioMicMute     spawn wpctl set-mute @DEFAULT_AUDIO_SOURCE@ toggle

# Brightness is a sysfs write, not brightnessctl or light -- neither is a
# dependency, and the file is group-writable to `video`. Change the device path
# here if yours is not intel_backlight.
#
# The floor of 1% of max is load-bearing: a backlight at 0 is a black screen,
# and the key that raises it again is one you can no longer see.
bind XF86MonBrightnessDown spawn "d=/sys/class/backlight/intel_backlight; m=$(cat $d/max_brightness); c=$(cat $d/brightness); lo=$((m/100+1)); n=$((c - m/20)); [ $n -gt $m ] && n=$m; [ $n -lt $lo ] && n=$lo; echo $n > $d/brightness"
bind XF86MonBrightnessUp   spawn "d=/sys/class/backlight/intel_backlight; m=$(cat $d/max_brightness); c=$(cat $d/brightness); lo=$((m/100+1)); n=$((c + m/20)); [ $n -gt $m ] && n=$m; [ $n -lt $lo ] && n=$lo; echo $n > $d/brightness"

# Mod+Alt+<letter> focuses a window whose app_id starts with that letter -- the
# one you used most recently, or the next one if you are already in that app.
# All 26 are generated from this one line. `app-keys none` drops the block.
app-keys Mod+Alt

# Apps that should get the keyboard to themselves: a VM console, a nested
# compositor, a remote desktop. While one of these has focus every binding above
# is disabled and the keys reach the client instead.
#
# Two bindings are never disabled, so you are never locked in: Mod+Shift+P
# (suspends passthrough for that one window) and Mod+Shift+E (exit). Whatever
# chords those two actions are bound to keep the exemption.
#
# app_ids are matched whole and case-insensitively. `foot -o ...` will not tell
# you one; run `satori` with the log open and read the `wm: window` lines, or
# check the app's desktop file.
#
# passthrough org.qemu.qemu virt-manager
# passthrough org.remmina.Remmina

# Some things you might want, that are not bound by default:
#
# bind Mod+Space none                    # unbind a built-in
# bind Print spawn grim -g "$(slurp)"    # needs grim + slurp
# bind Mod+Ctrl+G focus-app f            # re-point one letter by hand
